  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Daifugō. Japanese climbing game. Card order: 3→2→Joker (high). Play singles/pairs/triples/sequences higher than previous same-type combo. Four-of-a-kind = Revolution (inverts). First empty wins.',
    Player: "You hold: 3, 3, 5, 7, 7, 2, Joker. It's your lead—you can play the pair of 3s (weak but empties low cards), the pair of 7s (stronger), or the single Joker (nuclear option, wastes it). Best play: pair of 3s early—conserve 7s and Joker for responses to opponents' high pairs later.",
    objective: 'Be first to shed all cards (Daifugō = 1st place). Avoid being last (Daihinmin = last place).',
    gameplay: 'The player holding 3♦ (or 3♠ in some variants) leads first. On each turn, a player may:\n- Play a combination STRICTLY HIGHER than the previous combination of the SAME TYPE (single, pair, triple, sequence of 3+).\n- Or pass.\nThe played combination must match the current type (if singles are out, you must play singles). The round continues until all but one player passes; then the last player to play leads a new combination.',
    keyRules: [
      'You MUST match the current play type (singles vs. pairs vs. sequences).',
      'Revolution (four-of-a-kind) inverts rank order for rest of round.',
      'Daifugō-Daihinmin card exchange is the defining social mechanic.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Three to six players are typical; the game is usually played by 3 to 6, and up to 8 can take part (often with a single 53-card pack in Japan). With five or more players the full title hierarchy applies (Dai Fugō, Fugō, heimin, Hinmin, Dai Hinmin); with three or four only Fugō (winner) and Hinmin (loser) are used.',
      deck: 'Standard 52 cards + optional Jokers.',
      equipment: 'Cards.',
      dealing: 'Deal entire deck evenly. Players with extra cards start at a slight disadvantage.',
    },
    turnFlow: 'The player holding 3♦ (or 3♠ in some variants) leads first. On each turn, a player may:\n- Play a combination STRICTLY HIGHER than the previous combination of the SAME TYPE (single, pair, triple, sequence of 3+).\n- Or pass.\nThe played combination must match the current type (if singles are out, you must play singles). The round continues until all but one player passes; then the last player to play leads a new combination.',
